<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the files under Contents/Resources/psyched/ (the Gateway's Node runtime and its
  native addons) when the release is signed with Psyche's own self-signed certificate. Only two keys:

  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: V8 needs it under the hardened runtime.
  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: the Node runtime loads better-sqlite3,
    node-pty, sqlite-vec and onnxruntime as native addons. Without a team ID the hardened runtime
    refuses them ("different Team IDs") even though they carry the same certificate. Measured on
    macOS 27: the bundle check (psyched, with the bundle-check flag) fails with ERR_DLOPEN_FAILED without it and passes with it.

  What it costs, plainly: the same as for the app (entitlements.self-signed.plist). Malware running as
  the same user that can write inside a user-owned /Applications/Psyche.app can swap a dylib or addon
  inside the bundle, and it then runs in the Gateway's Node process, where the vault key is. The
  Electron fuses do not apply to this Node. Its launcher clears NODE_OPTIONS, NODE_PATH and
  NODE_REPL_EXTERNAL_MODULE and preloads psyched-harden.cjs so the per-user module folders are not
  searched, which stops injection from the environment and from the home folder, but not a write
  inside the bundle. The app's daily seal check covers that (bundle-integrity.ts). TCC access belongs
  to the app and to PsycheHelper.app, not to this process. A Developer ID build does not use this file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
